oll since August, most by mid-to-high single digits", "Biggs is weighed down by negative coverage and narrow appeal in a swing-leaning state", "Hobbs's roughly 3-to-1 fundraising edge and incumbency advantage", "Markets price Hobbs at 86-90%, though the newest surveys hint at tightening"], "rationale": "Polls since mid-August uniformly show Hobbs ahead, most by mid-to-high single digits, and betting markets price her as a strong favorite despite a tighter four-point reading in the latest survey. Biggs's nagging negative story and Hobbs's money and incumbency advantages outweigh the state's slight Republican lean and her debate-skipping controversy.", "watch": "If the tightening seen in the latest surveys continues, Hobbs's debate absence and the No Labels candidate's vote share could pull the race back toward tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Katie Hobbs (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's governor race pits incumbent Democrat Katie Hobbs against Republican Congressman Andy Biggs, with No Labels' Teri Hourihan and a Green candidate also on the ballot. Hobbs holds a large fundraising edge, having raised roughly $10 million to Biggs's $3 million, and polls since mid-August have consistently shown her ahead, with margins ranging from four points in the latest survey to double digits earlier in the fall.\n\nBiggs has trailed in every public poll, and coverage of his shifting account of a Colorado City bill has dogged his campaign in the final weeks. Hobbs drew criticism for skipping the October debates, and a potential split among Mormon women is being watched as a factor. Betting markets price Hobbs as a strong favorite, and rating outlets mostly call the race Lean Democratic, with one at Likely Democratic.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9144, "margin": 7.07}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9047, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9201, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9198, "marg